WHY AGPL AND NOT MIT

This repository was MIT licensed by default rather than by decision. MIT lets
anyone take this work, close it, and sell it, with no obligation to anyone.
That is not what was intended.

AGPL-3.0 is OSI-approved and genuinely open source: you may read, run, modify
and redistribute this software. What it adds is section 13 — if you run a
MODIFIED version of this software as a network service, you must offer your
users the complete corresponding source of your version, under this same
licence.

For a hosted web application that is the clause that matters. Someone may fork
this and compete; they may not fork it, close it, and compete.

WHY THE SOURCE IS PUBLIC AT ALL

Because the product makes claims that are worthless unless they can be checked:

  - that a signed trading bound is enforced by a pure function which fails
    closed, and refuses to open when the day's loss cannot be VERIFIED rather
    than merely read as zero (lib/cota/enforce.ts)
  - that the private note is sealed in the browser with a key derived from the
    hunter's passkey, and the server stores ciphertext it cannot open
  - that analytics never receives a wallet address, a transaction hash or an
    amount, and that URLs are redacted before they leave the browser
    (components/analytics/track.ts, components/analytics/route-name.ts)

Those are checkable here, by reading the code. That is the point of publishing
it.

